= Strategy — Wave 2

You have fought battles. You have won wars. You have built a kingdom from nothing. Now you must manage it — and expand it — at a scale where your personal sword matters less than your strategic mind.

== Endgame Warfare

The battles of Wave 2 are not skirmishes. They are campaigns involving thousands of troops across multiple armies and multiple fronts.

=== Multi-Army Strategy

At Clan Tier 4 and above, your military capacity is formidable. Your personal party, plus companion parties, plus allied lords summoned to your army, can easily exceed 1,000 troops in a single host. A thousand men under one banner is a hammer capable of shattering any single enemy army. But one hammer can only hit one target at a time, and the enemies of a mid-sized kingdom rarely oblige by presenting a single target.

The solution is a two-army doctrine. Army A, the Offensive Army, is led by you personally. It contains your best troops — your elite cavalry, your veteran archers, your Fian Champions and Imperial Legionaries that have survived a hundred battles. This army exists to capture territory. It sieges towns, smashes enemy field armies, and applies pressure where the enemy is weakest. It moves where you direct it and fights what you choose to fight.

Army B, the Defensive Army, is led by a trusted companion or a high-relation allied lord. Its composition is different — more infantry, more archers, less cavalry. Cavalry wins battles on the offensive. Infantry holds ground on the defensive. Army B patrols your border regions, responds to enemy raids, breaks small incursions before they become sieges, and holds threatened fiefs long enough for Army A to pivot back from its campaign.

The two-army doctrine has one non-negotiable rule: Army B must be large enough to survive contact with an enemy army of equal size for at least forty-eight in-game hours. If Army B has 400 troops and an enemy 600-man army crosses your border, Army B needs to hold them in a defensive siege long enough for you to march Army A back. If Army B is crushed in a single field battle, you return to find your border already overrun and the enemy sieging your interior towns. Size Army B proportionally to your most dangerous neighbor — if the Western Empire fields 800-man armies, your Defensive Army needs 500+ troops to buy you the march time home.

#tip-box[A kingdom that commits 100% of its military to offense will lose its home territory to enemy raids. The AI punishes undefended borders — enemy lords check your garrison strengths and attack the weakest. You do not need to match every border army man-for-man. You need one competent Defensive Army that can slow the enemy down until you, or a peace treaty, arrives.]

=== War Target Selection

Do not fight everyone at once. The kingdoms of Calradia will, given the opportunity, drag you into multi-front wars where your armies are stretched thin and your enemies converge. The only defense against this is choosing your wars based on clear, consistent criteria before you declare them.

*Geographic adjacency* is the first filter. Attack kingdoms that share a border with you. A war against a faction on the far side of the map means your armies march for seven days before they see an enemy, while your home territory sits undefended against your actual neighbors. Distant wars drain army cohesion, exhaust your vassals, and produce captured fiefs that are isolated enclaves impossible to defend. Every fief you take should be connected to territory you already hold. A kingdom shaped like Swiss cheese is a kingdom that loses territory every time someone sneezes.

*Enemy war exhaustion* is the second filter. Check the diplomacy screen before every war declaration. A faction already fighting two or more simultaneous wars is an easy target — its armies are already committed elsewhere, its lords are already wounded or captured, and its garrisons are already depleted. Your declaration is the tipping point that overwhelms their remaining capacity. A faction at peace with full garrisons and healthy armies is a much harder target. Patience is a strategic weapon: wait for the Southern Empire to overextend against the Khuzait, then strike when their eastern garrisons are empty.

*Settlement value* is the third filter. Not all conquests are equal. High-prosperity towns — 5,000 prosperity and above — are the prize. They generate tax revenue that funds your next war, they provide troop recruitment for your next army, and they project power across the surrounding region. Castles are secondary objectives at best. A castle controls a choke point or produces a resource, but it generates minimal income and requires constant garrison wages. Conquering a castle that costs 800 denars per day in garrison while earning 150 denars in taxes is a net loss. Take the towns first, then the castles that protect them.

*Choke points* are the fourth filter. Castles that sit on mountain passes, river crossings, or narrow coastal roads control movement across entire regions. A single castle at the right mountain pass can block an entire faction from entering your territory. Prioritize these over castles in open plains — a castle you cannot bypass is worth more than three castles you can ride around.

=== Sustaining an Offensive

The biggest challenge in endgame warfare is not winning battles — it is keeping your army in the field long enough to win the war. A battle lasts twenty minutes. A siege lasts two weeks. The logistics of sustaining an army for two weeks in enemy territory will determine whether your campaign succeeds.

*Food supply* is your primary constraint. A 1,000-man army consumes roughly 20 to 30 units of food per day depending on composition — cavalry-heavy armies eat more because horses consume grain. Your party carries a food reserve that depletes continuously, and when it hits zero, your troops begin to starve. Starving troops take morale penalties, then desert. A starving army can lose 50 men per day to desertion, and those men take their expensive equipment with them. The siege that you were winning on day eight becomes unwinnable on day twelve because half your army walked away.

The solution is a three-part supply chain. First, assign a high-Steward companion as your Quartermaster — Steward skill directly reduces food consumption rate, and a 200-Steward Quartermaster can stretch the same food supply to feed 30% more troops. Second, capture enemy villages near your siege for emergency food — a single village provides 50 to 100 units of food, enough for several days of operation. Third, understand seasonal effects: winter multiplies food consumption across the board. Besieging a town in midwinter is a race against your own stomach. Besieging in autumn, when harvests are in and food is abundant, gives you the margin you need.

*Reinforcement pipelines* are your second constraint. Sieges cost lives. Even a successful siege assault against an undermanned garrison will kill 30 to 80 of your troops — more if the defenders are reinforced or the walls are undamaged. You need a way to replace those losses without marching back to your capital. Maintain garrisons of trained mid-tier troops in your core fiefs — 50 to 100 men per fief, culture-matched to your army composition. When you need reinforcements, assign a companion party to ferry troops from your core fiefs to the front. The companion rides back, collects troops from multiple garrisons, and returns. This pipeline takes five to ten in-game days depending on distance, so plan resupply before your army reaches critical depletion.

*Rotation of wounded* is the third constraint. After every major battle, a significant fraction of your troops are wounded, occupying party slots but unable to fight. Transfer wounded to a companion party and send them to the nearest friendly town to recover while you press the offensive with healthy troops. The companion returns with fresh troops from garrison reserves, completing the rotation cycle. This keeps your offensive army at full combat strength while casualties recover behind the lines.

#warning-box[Sieging in enemy territory is the single most dangerous logistics challenge in Bannerlord. You are stationary for up to two weeks while enemy armies maneuver. You need enough food to outlast the defenders' supplies — a town with a full Granary can hold out for 30 days or more. If your food runs out before the town's, you lose the siege, your army starves, and the enemy relief force catches you retreating with half morale and no supplies. Budget food for the full expected siege duration plus a 50% safety margin.]

== Kingdom Management

Congratulations — you are the ruler. Every problem in the kingdom is now your problem.

=== Vassal Management

Vassals are the muscle of your kingdom. Without vassals, your kingdom is a single clan fighting a continent, and a single clan cannot be everywhere at once. With vassals, your kingdom operates multiple armies, patrols multiple borders, and prosecutes multiple wars. The quality of your vassal management determines whether your kingdom is a coalition or a collection of malcontents waiting to leave.

Grant fiefs after sieges, and grant them to the vassals who contributed troops to the siege effort. The participation score — calculated from troop count, siege engine contribution, and time spent in the siege camp — determines who the game considers most deserving. Following this score keeps your vassals content. Overruling it to give the fief to yourself or a favorite costs influence and relation. Overruling it repeatedly signals to your vassals that their contributions do not matter, and vassals who feel unappreciated start looking for a new liege.

Do not hoard fiefs. A landed vassal is a productive vassal — they upgrade their holdings, garrison their borders, and generate income that funds their own war parties. A landless vassal is restless. If a vassal has zero fiefs and the kingdom has gone three wars without granting them one, they will defect. The relationship formula is simple: one fief equals content; three fiefs equals loyal for life; zero fiefs equals a defection timer counting down.

Support pro-vassal policies while your position as ruler is still consolidating. Noble Retinues, Council of Commons, and Citizenship all benefit vassals directly, and a vassal who feels their king supports their interests is less likely to join a rebellion. Once your kingdom is stable and your treasury is full, you can reconsider policies that consolidate power to the crown — but early in your reign, the crown's power is the sum of its vassals' loyalty.

Vote with your vassals on decisions they care about, even when you have the influence to override them. Every vote cast against a vassal costs relation with that vassal's entire clan. A vote cast with them builds relation. Relation is a resource that prevents defection, accelerates army recruitment, and determines whether vassals support your fief claims. Spend it only when the outcome is critical. If you do not care who gets a minor border castle, vote with the majority and bank the relation.

#danger-box[Vassals defecting during a war can turn a winning campaign into a losing one overnight. A vassal clan that leaves takes its fiefs with it — those fiefs become enemy territory, your border collapses, and the enemy suddenly has a new ally who knows exactly where your garrisons are weakest. Monitor relation levels of border vassals every few in-game weeks. A vassal whose relationship drops below -20 should be your top diplomatic priority — grant them a fief, support their policy preferences, or marry a family member into their clan to restore the buffer zone.]

=== Internal Stability

A kingdom that is not stable internally cannot fight wars externally. Internal stability is governed primarily by settlement loyalty, and loyalty is governed by a set of mechanics you must manage across every fief in your realm.

Loyalty is affected by five primary factors. *Culture match* between the settlement and its owner and/or governor — each matching culture grants +1 loyalty per day. A Battanian-owned Battanian-culture town with a Battanian governor enjoys +2 loyalty per day from culture match alone. An Imperial-owned Battanian town with an Aserai governor suffers -2 loyalty per day. *Security*, representing the garrison's ability to suppress unrest — the larger the garrison relative to the settlement's population, the higher the security bonus. A fully garrisoned town of 200 troops against a population of 5,000 generates a security bonus of roughly +1 to +2 loyalty. *Policies* — certain kingdom policies directly affect loyalty (Citizenship grants +0.5, Imperial Towns penalizes -1). *Recent ownership* — a newly captured settlement suffers a -3 loyalty penalty that decays over approximately thirty in-game days as the populace adjusts to their new ruler. *Starvation* — zero food in a settlement causes rapid loyalty decay, roughly -1 per day, which will trigger rebellion within weeks if unresolved.

Rebellions trigger when loyalty drops below a critical threshold — approximately 15 to 20 — and remains there for an extended period. When a rebellion triggers, the town or castle declares independence and spawns a rebel army roughly equal in size to the garrison plus the local militia. The settlement becomes a hostile minor faction, and you must reconquer it. A reconquered settlement starts at zero loyalty with the full -3 recent ownership penalty, meaning a second rebellion is nearly guaranteed unless you invest aggressively in recovery.

Prevent rebellion through a layered approach. First, assign a culture-matched governor — this is the single most impactful action and costs nothing except one companion. Second, build Fairgrounds — the +1 loyalty bonus accelerates construction of everything else. Third, maintain an adequate garrison — set an auto-recruitment cap of roughly 100 to 150 troops for an interior town, 200 to 250 for a border town. Fourth, fund the Festival event during the crisis period after conquest — festivals convert denars into temporary loyalty boosts, expensive but essential when a settlement is hovering at 10 loyalty and you cannot survive a rebellion. Fifth, avoid stacking negative-loyalty policies. If your kingdom has Imperial Towns and Debasement of Currency active simultaneously, every town in your realm is fighting a -2 loyalty penalty before any positive modifiers apply.

#warning-box[Multi-front wars cripple your ability to suppress rebellions. A rebellion in a border town during a three-front war is an existential crisis — you cannot pull an army away from any front without losing territory on that front, but if you ignore the rebellion, the town declares independence and becomes a fourth front. The safe approach is to ensure every fief has at least +2 net loyalty before you commit to a multi-front campaign. If you are capturing territory faster than you can stabilize it, you are losing.]

=== Vassal Defection Prevention

Vassal defection is governed by a three-pillar system that determines a clan's willingness to remain in your kingdom.

The first pillar is *relation*. Your personal relationship with each vassal clan leader is the primary factor. A relation of +50 or above is the safe zone — vassals at this level will not defect even during prolonged wars or after multiple fief disputes. A relation between -20 and +50 is the neutral zone — vassals will stay as long as conditions are favorable but may defect if the war turns against you or if they go too long without a fief grant. A relation below -20 is the danger zone — these vassals are actively looking for an opportunity to leave, and they will find one within a few in-game months.

The second pillar is *fief count*. One to three fiefs is the content range — the vassal has enough land to generate income and feel invested in the kingdom. Zero fiefs is restless — within a few months, a landless vassal will announce their intention to leave, and you will have a short window (roughly a week) to grant them a fief before they defect. Four or more fiefs makes a vassal essentially permanent — they have so much invested in the kingdom that leaving would cost them more than staying could ever gain.

The third pillar is *shared enemies*. Vassals gain relation with you for every battle you fight together against the same enemy. If the kingdom is at war with Vlandia and you personally fight alongside a vassal's army against Vlandian lords, that vassal's relation with you rises. Conversely, vassals who never see you on the battlefield feel disconnected from the kingdom. A ruler who sits in the capital while vassals fight the wars loses relation through neglect.

Beyond the three pillars, marriage provides a permanent relation buffer. Marry your sister to a vassal's son, and that vassal's entire clan gains a permanent +10 to +20 relation with you that never decays. This is the nuclear option for securing a wavering vassal — expensive in political capital, but permanent. Multiple marriages across multiple vassal clans create a web of relations that makes mass defection nearly impossible. A vassal whose sister is married to your brother will not defect unless conditions are catastrophic.

#tip-box[Non-aggression pacts and trade agreements do not affect vassal loyalty. They are agreements between kingdoms, not bonds within one. A vassal protected by a non-aggression pact with the neighboring kingdom will still defect to that kingdom if their relation with you is bad enough.]

== Advanced Diplomacy

=== Alliance Networks

Mutual defense pacts — alliances — coordinate your wars with allied kingdoms. When you ally with another kingdom, your wars become their wars and vice versa. When Vlandia declares war on you, your Battanian allies declare war on Vlandia automatically. When the Northern Empire attacks the Battanians, you are automatically at war with the Northern Empire. Alliance networks can transform a losing war into an even fight and an even fight into a rout — but they come with costs.

The primary cost of an alliance is entanglement in wars you did not choose. If your ally is aggressive, an alliance with them means constant war. Each declaration by your ally drags your kingdom into a conflict that may be on the far side of the map, against an enemy who has no quarrel with you, at a time when you desperately need peace to recover from your last campaign. You cannot refuse — the alliance treaty obligates you to honor the war declaration, and refusing breaks the alliance with massive relation penalties and a long cooldown before you can ally again.

The secondary cost is the political friction of alliance. When you are allied to Battania and the Western Empire simultaneously, and those two allies go to war with each other, you must choose which alliance to honor and which to break. You will lose one ally, and the kingdom you abandon will remember it for a generation.

The strategic rule of alliances is simple: only ally with kingdoms you share a border with. A distant ally cannot send armies to help you — the march time means their armies arrive after your war is already won or lost. But a distant ally can drag you into their regional wars, forcing you to march your armies across the map while your borders sit undefended. Border allies reinforce each other in days, not weeks. Their wars are your wars geographically, not just diplomatically.

#tip-box[Alliance with a neighboring kingdom that shares your primary enemy is the most valuable diplomatic relationship in the game. If you border Vlandia and the Battanians border Vlandia, an alliance means Vlandia fights a two-front war every time they declare on either of you. The AI cannot handle two-front wars effectively — their armies will shuttle between fronts and accomplish nothing on either.]

=== Strategic Betrayals

Betrayal is a tool. It has a cost that must be weighed against its gains, and the cost compounds — each betrayal makes the next one more expensive.

The optimal moment to betray an ally or break a non-aggression pact is when the target is already at war with multiple factions. Check the diplomacy screen. If the Western Empire is fighting the Northern Empire, the Battanians, and the Vlandians simultaneously, their armies are already committed on three fronts. Your declaration is the fourth front they cannot cover. Their border towns are ungarrisoned. Their lords are captured or recovering. Your armies sweep through their territory before they can organize resistance. This is the betrayal that wins a war in weeks.

The second window of vulnerability opens immediately after a peace treaty. Garrisons are depleted from the war just ended. Armies are dispersed and recovering. Lords are wounded. The kingdom is not expecting a new war — the AI's threat assessment resets after peace, and border garrisons take weeks to rebuild. Declaring war in this window means you fight a weakened enemy on your schedule, not theirs.

The cost of betrayal is reputation damage across all factions, not just your target. Every faction tracks your reliability as a diplomatic partner. Betray one ally, and future alliance proposals cost more influence, require higher starting relations, and face more council opposition. Betray two, and kingdoms that previously accepted non-aggression pacts now refuse. Betray three, and you are diplomatically isolated — no one will ally, no one will sign a pact, and every neighbor assumes you will eventually betray them. Betrayal is a weapon with limited ammunition. Use it for wars that matter, not for every minor border skirmish.

=== Non-Aggression Pacts

Non-aggression pacts are defensive diplomacy. A pact with a neighboring kingdom means that kingdom will not declare war on you for the pact's duration. This does not prevent you from declaring war on them — the pact only restricts the AI's behavior, not yours. If you declare on a kingdom with whom you have a pact, the pact is broken and you suffer the same reputation penalty as any betrayal.

The primary use of non-aggression pacts is securing one border while you expand on another. A kingdom bordered by three factions on three sides needs to fight them one at a time. A pact with the faction on your western border means your western territory is safe while your armies campaign east. When the eastern campaign is complete and that border is stable, you can pivot west — the pact will have expired or you can break it when you are ready to fight on that front.

Pacts cost influence to propose and must pass a council vote. The influence cost is moderate — comparable to proposing a policy — and the council vote is usually favorable if the target kingdom is not a traditional enemy of your faction. Pacts with traditional enemies (Vlandia and Battania, or Empire and Khuzait) may fail council votes repeatedly.

== Succession Planning

=== Heirs

Children born from your marriage become playable clan members after approximately 18 in-game years pass. This is a multi-decade investment — the child you produce in year five of your campaign will not become playable until year twenty-three. In a standard-length campaign where total victory is achieved in ten to fifteen in-game years, you will never play as your child unless you deliberately extend the game past its natural conclusion.

Education matters during the waiting period. Assign a guardian companion to each child — the child inherits skill experience multipliers from their guardian, biasing their development toward the guardian's strengths. A child assigned to a 200-Steward guardian will develop Steward faster than other skills. A child assigned to a 200-Tactics guardian will favor military development. Choose guardians strategically based on the role you want each child to play when they come of age — one heir for stewardship and governance, one spare for military command.

#tip-box[Children are a multi-decade investment. Do not expect to play as them unless you are running an exceptionally long campaign or using mods that accelerate the aging mechanic. Treat children as a roleplaying element, a succession safety net in case your main character dies with death enabled, and a source of marriage alliances. Their strategic impact is generational, not immediate.]

=== Clan Longevity

A clan with multiple adult family members is a clan that survives disasters. If your main character dies in battle with clan member death enabled, you continue playing as your spouse or an adult family member. A clan that consists of one character and seven companions dies permanently when that character falls. A clan with a spouse, two adult siblings, and three adult children has five backup protagonists and can lose characters without losing the game.

Spread marriage alliances across multiple factions. Marry your brother to a Vlandian clan, your sister to a Battanian one, and your heir to an Imperial noble house. When one faction betrays you, the other two marriages may preserve those alliances. When Vlandia declares war, your Battanian in-laws vote against the declaration because they do not want to fight their sister's kingdom. Marriage networks create diplomatic cushioning that absorbs shocks before they become crises.

=== Clan Longevity

Multiple family members mean multiple party leaders, multiple governors, and multiple backup characters if your main one dies. Each adult family member can lead a party — your spouse leads one army while you lead another, and your adult siblings lead companion-tier parties. Each family member can govern a fief with culture-matching bonuses. A large clan is a distributed military and administrative apparatus, not just a collection of relatives.

Spread marriage alliances strategically across factions. A brother married to a Vlandian clan and a sister married to a Battanian one creates diplomatic redundancy. When Vlandia betrays you, the Battanian alliance may hold. When one marriage burns, another marriage may save you. This is long-term insurance — marriages take years to arrange and decades to pay dividends, but a clan that has been building marriage ties for two generations is a clan that cannot be isolated.

== Total War Logistics

=== Food Supply Chains

Army-scale consumption is the limiting factor on every campaign. A 1,000-man army eats between 20 and 30 units of food per day depending on composition — cavalry armies eat more, infantry armies eat less. Your party's food inventory depletes continuously at this rate. A captured village provides a one-time food injection of roughly 50 to 100 units, enough for two to five days of operations. After the village is stripped, you need a new food source or you need to retreat.

Plan resupply points every three to four days of marching. This means identifying villages along your campaign route that will still have food when you arrive — villages that the enemy has not already stripped, villages in regions where crops have ripened, villages that are not currently under siege. A campaign plan without resupply points is a starvation plan.

Seasonal effects are decisive. Autumn is the season of abundance — crops have been harvested, villages are full, and food is cheap and plentiful. Siege in autumn if you have a choice. Winter multiplies food consumption by roughly 1.5x, making every day of winter campaigning exponentially more expensive in supplies. Besieging in winter is a race against your own logistics — if the siege lasts more than seven days in deep winter, your army will starve before the town surrenders. Summer and spring are transitional — less food than autumn, less penalty than winter, the baseline for campaign planning.

#warning-box[Do not besiege in winter unless you have overwhelming food reserves and a Quartermaster with 200+ Steward. The food penalty is multiplicative — the same siege that costs 300 food in autumn costs 450+ food in winter. If you misjudge, your army starves, the siege fails, and you lose weeks of campaign progress.]

=== Reinforcement Pipelines

Wars are won by the last army standing, not the first army that attacks. Maintaining combat strength across a campaign requires a reinforcement pipeline that delivers fresh troops to the front without requiring the commander to return to home territory.

Companion parties function as couriers. Send a companion from your offensive army back to your core fiefs. The companion stops at each of your garrison towns and transfers troops from the garrison to their party. When full, the companion returns to the front and merges with your army. The time cost is the round-trip march time — five to ten in-game days from a distant front to your capital and back. This means you must anticipate losses four to six days before they become critical and send the courier early.

Garrison rotation is the second mechanism. Maintain garrisons of trained troops — not raw recruits — in your core fiefs. Fifty to 100 mid-tier troops per fief, culture-matched to your army composition. These garrisons are your strategic reserve. When a frontline fief is threatened, transfer troops from the reserve to the front. When a frontline fief is quiet, transfer surplus garrison troops back to the reserve. The rotation keeps your best troops where the fighting is hottest.

Troop training time is the hidden cost. A trained troop is the product of weeks of recruitment, upgrade, and combat survival. Every troop lost in a siege or a field battle is weeks of production time destroyed in minutes. The reinforcement pipeline must be producing and stockpiling troops during peacetime so that you have reserves when war starts. A kingdom that only starts recruiting when war is declared fights its first campaign with raw recruits against veteran enemies.

=== War Exhaustion

Even victorious wars have a cost, and the cost is measured in influence, morale, and vassal patience — the three currencies of war exhaustion.

*Influence drain* is the most immediate cost. Raising and maintaining armies costs influence per day of cohesion. A 1,000-man army on a two-week siege campaign costs 30 to 50 influence in cohesion alone. Summoning additional lords costs more. Proposing continued war when the council wants peace costs influence to override. When your kingdom's influence pool runs dry, no one can form armies, no one can propose policies, and the kingdom drifts aimlessly while enemies retake lost territory. Watch the kingdom's collective influence before committing to extended campaigns.

*Troop morale* degrades during long campaigns. Morale decays faster when food is low, when casualties are high, and when the army has been away from home territory for extended periods. Low morale reduces combat effectiveness — troops route faster and fight with less damage. An army that has been on campaign for six continuous months has lower morale than an army that is fresh out of garrison rest. Rotate armies — send one army home to recover morale while a fresh army takes over the front.

*Vassal fatigue* is the political cost of constant war. Vassals want peacetime to manage their fiefs, upgrade their buildings, and recover their party strength. A vassal whose party has been at war for twelve straight months has not had time to upgrade their town, train their garrison, or pursue personal objectives. Vassals who are perpetually at war accumulate resentment and become more likely to defect. The cycle is: six to twelve months of war for territorial gains, three to six months of peace for recovery and consolidation. This cycle keeps vassals content, armies strong, and influence pools full. A kingdom that fights continuously for two years will face mass defections regardless of how many wars it is winning.

== Map-Painting Strategy

=== Conquest Order

The order in which you conquer your enemies matters more than how fast you conquer them. The strategic priority is: weakest faction first, strongest faction last.

Eliminate minor and weakened factions first. A faction that has already lost half its territory in previous wars is fighting from a disadvantage — low clan tier, depleted garrisons, fewer lords. Finish what their previous enemies started. Eliminating a faction completely removes them from the diplomatic map: no more war declarations from that direction, no more border to defend, no more potential allies for your remaining enemies. Every faction eliminated is a border removed from your strategic calculations.

Encirclement is the second principle. Capture territory that surrounds your remaining enemies. A faction whose territory is completely encircled by your kingdom cannot recruit from outside their borders, cannot trade without passing through your towns, and cannot send armies anywhere without walking through your territory. Encirclement turns every subsequent war into a siege of their remaining holdings — they cannot relieve each other, cannot reinforce, and cannot escape.

The absolute rule: never fight a two-front war by choice. If circumstances force a two-front war — an ally declares war while you are already committed — prioritize finishing one front before engaging the other. Half-measures on both fronts lose both. A kingdom that commits 60% of its forces to one front and 40% to the other will lose on both. The correct play is 100% commitment to the more dangerous front, accept territorial losses on the secondary front, and pivot when the primary enemy is broken.

=== Choke Points

Specific castles control movement across entire regions. A castle built at a mountain pass — the passes connecting Battania to Vlandia, the narrow corridors between the Empire and the Aserai desert — blocks the only path an army can take. An enemy faction whose access to your territory is blocked by a mountain-pass castle has exactly two options: siege the castle, or not enter your territory. There is no third option.

River bridges function similarly. A castle at a bridge crossing forces armies to either attack the castle or find another crossing — and another crossing may be days of marching away in the wrong direction. A well-garrisoned bridge castle can delay an entire invasion by a week, buying you the time to redeploy your offensive army to the threatened sector.

Garrison choke points heavily. A castle that controls a mountain pass or a bridge needs 200+ troops, not the 60 a standard garrison would hold. The cost in garrison wages is the cost of border security — compare 800 denars per day for a heavily garrisoned choke point against losing three interior towns to enemy raids because the enemy bypassed a lightly garrisoned position. The math favors the heavy garrison every time.

=== Vassal Distribution

Where you place your vassals' fiefs determines how effectively your kingdom can defend itself. Assign border fiefs to military-focused clans. A clan whose leader has high Tactics, Leadership, and combat skills will naturally produce and lead larger parties, patrol their border territory, and respond to raids without your direct orders. Their personal combat effectiveness translates into border security for your kingdom.

Assign interior fiefs to economy-focused clans. A clan whose leader has high Steward, Trade, and Charm will maximize prosperity, tax revenue, and construction speed in their holdings. Their economic output funds the military clans on the border. Interior vassals produce the money — border vassals spend it on defense.

Give neighboring fiefs to the same clan whenever possible. A clan that holds three adjacent castles and a town in the same region will defend that region as a unit. Their parties patrol the contiguous territory. Their garrisons reinforce each other. An enemy cannot pick off one fief without engaging the entire clan's military apparatus. Conversely, never spread one clan's holdings across the map. A clan with one fief in Vlandia, one in Sturgia, and one in the Aserai desert cannot defend any of them. Their parties spend all their time marching between holdings and never actually fight anyone.

This distribution strategy scales: as your kingdom conquers new territory, assign the border vassals to the new border and move the now-safe interior vassals to produce economy. The vassal who held the western border against Vlandia for five years earns the right to a peaceful interior town when Vlandia is conquered and the border moves further west.
